WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.880
- So, thank you so much for joining us

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.700
for Gaps in Health Equity,
Insights From History,

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.200
Lessons From COVID-19,
and Ideas for the Future.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.200
This is the 10th in a series,

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.790
the Yale Development Dialogues,

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.030
which are virtual panel
discussions hosted by

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:26.720
Yale's Economic Growth Center,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.720
Jackson Institute for Global Affairs

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.300
and History Department.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.170
I'm Catherine Cheney.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:32.350
I'm a Reporter at Devex

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.330
focused on technology and
innovation and global development.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.020
And I really enjoy moderating
these conversations.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.740
We bring together perspectives
from history, economics,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.640
and policy to discuss
ways to address challenges

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.740
facing low and middle income countries.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.630
And today we're joined by
experts who are leading voices

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.930
in understanding health and
healthcare challenges globally.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.080
So, I have a number of
questions I'd like to ask them,

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.090
but for those of you
who are joining us Live

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.130
here on the Zoom webinar,

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:58.920
we really encourage you
to submit your questions

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.420
via the Q&amp;A function.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.180
And please don't wait until the very end

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.160
so that I can have a sense
of what you wanna hear

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:07.180
and make the conversation as
useful for you as possible.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.960
Transitioning to the topic
that we're tackling today.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.440
We all know that unequal
access to the COVID-19 vaccine

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.440
has magnified what are
really long standing gaps

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.930
in health equity.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.090
And I found this
definition of health equity

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:21.800
from the Robert Wood Johnson Foundation

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:24.350
to be really useful in
preparing for this conversation.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.027
So I'll share it with you.

00:01:26.027 --> 00:01:28.790
"Health equity means
that everyone has a fair

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:32.090
and just opportunity to
be as healthy as possible.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.440
This requires removing
obstacles to health,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.550
such as poverty, discrimination
and their consequences,

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:40.750
including powerlessness and
lack of access to good jobs

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.800
with fair pay, quality
education and housing,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.350
safe environments and healthcare."

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.360
So structural power inequities,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.170
which the COVID-19 pandemic again,

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.240
has really highlighted results
in different health outcomes

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.720
based on factors that are
outside of people's control,

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.550
like where they were born.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:00.150
In this webinar will
explore whether this crisis

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:03.340
will in fact, change the
conversation on health equity

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:06.330
and propel the kinds of
partnerships needed to achieve it.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.880
We'll ask what lessons we can draw

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.570
from the COVID 19 pandemic
and from the past,

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:13.400
in order to design a
more equitable future.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.010
I wanna quickly introduce our panelists

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.570
who can share more with us.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:19.390
Today, I'm joined by Anne-Emanuelle Birn,

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:21.520
who's Professor of Critical
Development Studies

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.690
and of Social and
Behavioral Health Sciences

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.080
at the Dalla Lana School of Public Health

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.424
at University of Toronto.

00:02:28.424 --> 00:02:31.260
Pascaline Dupas is the
Professor of Economics

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.870
at Stanford University
and Faculty Director

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.480
of the Stanford King Center
on Global Development,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.610
which also has fantastic
events they've put on.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.510
I highly recommend them.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:44.000
Dr. Mesfin Teklu Tessema
is Head of the Health Unit

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.000
at the international Rescue Committee

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.980
and IRC Senior Leader in Health.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.680
And Sunil Amrith is the Renu
and Anand Dhawan Professor

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:53.890
of History at Yale,

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:55.900
and one of three co-organizers

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.620
of the Yale Development Dialogue series

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.650
together with Rahini Pande, Rory Stewart.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:02.850
Thanks so much to all
of you for joining us.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:05.550
So, I wanna begin with you, Sunil.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.640
I mean, you've really
championed this topic

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:08.960
as one that we should take on

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:11.060
as part of the Yale
Development Dialogue series,

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.340
bringing these different
perspectives together.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.240
And I'd love for you to expand a bit on

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.937
why this topic and why now.

00:03:18.236 --> 00:03:21.450
- Thank you, Catherine, and
thank you all for joining us.

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.620
Last year, when we started the
Yale Development Dialogues,

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.780
we were really in the
very midst of the pandemic

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.330
as we still are in many ways.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.010
And we had a couple of very
successful conversations

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:35.340
on COVID, on COVID and
inequality, COVID and development.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.920
And I think one of the messages
that was loud and clear

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.320
to me from those conversations was

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.270
this is only the beginning
of the conversation

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:46.223
that if anything, COVID has
highlighted just how deep rooted

00:03:46.223 --> 00:03:49.630
inequities and health
are around the world.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.760
As a historian, I've been very interested

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.760
in the history of moves
towards health equity,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.200
both as a regional and a global scale.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.600
And I think what we've seen is that

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.550
there are periods when there have been

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:08.703
substantive moves towards
a greater health equity.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.340
You see this after the Second World War

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.840
where despite mass killing there was also

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:19.850
technological innovation and
process of decolonization

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.610
to the point where with
the foundation of the WHO

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:26.030
with the disease eradication campaigns

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:27.300
though they were flawed,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.820
they were in some sense
profoundly democratizing

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.520
this was to go back to the definition

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.550
you gave us Catherine about
removing some of the obstacles

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.720
to the majority of the world's population

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.220
having better conditions of health.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.340
See a very different
movement in the 1970s,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.490
which is really coming
from the Global South.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:46.580
And this is the movement that leads

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:47.727
to that very famous slogan,

00:04:47.727 --> 00:04:50.087
"Health for all by the year 2000".

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.930
In the 2000s I think we see
a different type of movement

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.610
that kind of philanthropic movement

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:59.003
around the formation of
Garvey and the Global Fund.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.820
Each of these movements has
its moment and of course,

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:05.950
we can talk about the
weaknesses within each of them,

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:08.150
which explains why
perhaps they didn't have

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.110
the impact that they intended to.

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:11.370
I think there are three questions

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.060
that come out of looking
at those histories though.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.340
One is the question of accountability.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:20.920
I think we always come
back to this question of

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.360
who's responsible for health equity?

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.160
Who has in a sense, the
right to make these decisions

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:29.030
about what will remove the obstacles

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:30.690
to the majority of the world,

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.550
having better access to
conditions of health?

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.840
The second is this constant push and pull

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.620
between a bottom up and
a top down approach.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:42.180
One of the criticisms of the
disease eradication campaigns

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.310
of the 50s but also
some of the more recent

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.640
philanthropic efforts is that

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:47.830
they've been very top-down,

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.440
not really consultative,
not really responsive

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.030
to what people need in different
parts of the Global South.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:56.460
And then the third question is,

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.120
how does one build political support

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.630
to make health equity a priority,

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:04.440
not just at the level
of global institutions,

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.530
but in each individual country

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.780
where this is a challenge.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.960
and that includes right
here in the United States.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.500
So I'm very keen to
hear from our panelists

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.543
who have such a diverse
range of expertise,

00:06:18.543 --> 00:06:21.103
their perspectives on
all of these question.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:22.843
- Thank you, Sunil.

00:06:22.843 --> 00:06:24.410
Really helpful framing here.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:27.520
And I appreciate your point that

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.750
we need to look to history
and evaluate what worked

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.530
and what didn't in each
of these movements.

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:34.960
Why didn't they have
their intended impacts?

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:38.640
We once talked about health
for all by the year 2000,

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.540
here we are in 2021.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:42.040
And the sustainable development goals

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:43.940
are one of these frameworks looking at

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:47.340
really health for all by 2030.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.173
Will we get there?

00:06:48.173 --> 00:06:49.780
What will it take and what
can we learn from history?

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:52.290
So really helpful framing.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:54.670
Building on what we can learn from history

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:56.950
I'd love to bring
Anne-Emanuelle into this.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:59.110
So, your work focuses on the history

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:01.540
and politics of international
and global health,

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:03.170
mostly in Latin America.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:06.930
And I wonder if you can
expand on lessons from history

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:09.113
when it comes to advancing health equity.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:14.100
- Sure. And let me begin by saying that

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.760
I don't quite agree with the
notion of lessons from history.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.750
I think that perspectives, shedding light,

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:25.960
contextualizing,
understanding are better ways

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:29.480
because the context always shifts, right?

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.920
So you can't have importable
or exportable lessons,

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:36.010
whether over time or over space,

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:40.310
just as some of the very
prescriptive top-down

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:42.610
health campaigns that Sunil was mentioning

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:47.610
are very fraught when Peru is
seen as the same as Thailand

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.230
is seen as the same as
Botswana, et cetera.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:57.170
So I just wanted to put that
on the table to get us going.

00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:58.340
- I appreciate that.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:03.340
- And in terms of the Latin
American region historically,

00:08:05.358 --> 00:08:09.460
I think we also need to consider

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:14.460
moments of advance progress,
but also enormous setbacks

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:21.070
not necessarily the same
or expected moments in time

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:25.530
in terms of international events.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:30.530
So there's also very peculiar
or specific situations

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:32.900
in different regions.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:37.900
And one issue has to do with the rise

00:08:38.138 --> 00:08:40.390
of Social Security systems.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:44.540
Actually fairly early on in
many Latin American countries

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:45.800
that were industrializing,

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:48.490
Chile as one of the prime examples.

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:52.030
And so there was an early
Social Security system

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:56.880
in 1924 set up, but it was exclusive to

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:58.580
urban industrial workers,

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.510
not even their families were included.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:03.220
And so you had this step forward,

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:05.830
but then the exclusion
of indigenous people,

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:08.210
of rural peoples, et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:11.770
And then you have another wave in the...

00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:15.290
Well, expectation of
wave in the late 1930s

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:18.340
when the popular front is elected

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:23.340
and Salvador Allende becomes
Chile Minister of Health.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.560
And yet the expectation of setting up

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:29.610
a universal healthcare
system fails because

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:34.150
it gets embroiled in some of
the anticommunist ideology

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:39.510
and pressures in the 1940s.

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.060
So you really have these waves.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:45.120
Then in the 1950s, the
leftists are out of government

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:50.120
and they do manage to put a
universal healthcare system

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:53.790
in place with certain flaws
and exclusions and so on.

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:56.850
And so we need to think
about this heterogeneity

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:59.630
as well as kind of narrative flipping,

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:03.240
which I'm always concerned
with this idea that

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.080
countries of the Global
South are following,

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.410
are learning in the Bismarckian Model

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:11.000
or the British National
Health Service Model.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.210
In fact, Chileans would argue the opposite

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:15.170
that the Brits just got faster

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:20.053
to the legislative sausage
making than the Chileans did.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:25.920
But also the importance
of not having nostalgia

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:27.180
for the past.

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:32.180
So, no society has ever
reached perfect health equity.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.200
It's always a process, right?

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.050
And so even countries that
have made enormous strides,

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:43.820
such as Cuba have been slower
in some realms than others.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.330
I mean, they've worked
very hard to bring in

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:49.010
rural folks and prioritizing rural folks,

00:10:49.010 --> 00:10:53.507
but rather later at bringing LGTBQIA folks

00:10:56.689 --> 00:10:58.980
having their health needs covered.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:03.980
So I think there's kind of
a wide heterogeneity there

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:08.430
that we need to be mindful
of when looking at history

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:11.810
and thinking through those perspectives.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:13.630
- Yes, I really appreciate the nuance

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:15.220
you've brought to this.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:17.610
Both that no country has
reached perfect health equity,

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:18.810
it's always a process.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:21.828
And that you can't have these

00:11:21.828 --> 00:11:25.100
importable exportable
lessons context matters.

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:26.100
I think that's really valuable.

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:27.080
Thank you.

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:28.550
I wanna bring Pascaline into this.

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:30.600
So your research has focused on questions

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:31.940
related to health equity.

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:33.510
There are many examples of this

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:34.780
one being for example,

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:36.330
why there hasn't been more investment

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:38.640
in fighting neglected diseases.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:41.570
And I wonder if you can expand
on some of your research

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:43.180
and some of your findings most relevant

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:44.730
to this topic of health equity.

00:11:48.411 --> 00:11:51.470
- So my own research is not so much about

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:54.194
why some pieces are neglected

00:11:54.194 --> 00:11:56.840
I think we know exactly
why they are neglected.

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:59.870
It's just a matter of like
economics, unfortunately,

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:01.870
which is that all of the diseases

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:03.990
that are specific to really poor countries

00:12:05.124 --> 00:12:07.590
the pharmaceutical companies
would have the incentives

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:10.500
to do R&amp;D, well there's no incentive there

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:12.730
because the very people
who need this medicine,

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:16.070
all these vaccines cannot
afford to pay for them.

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:17.357
And they can't afford to
pay very much for them.

00:12:17.357 --> 00:12:19.140
And their governments cannot afford

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:20.440
to pay very much for them.

00:12:21.532 --> 00:12:23.550
Unfortunately where the economics

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:26.760
versus health comes into play.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:30.080
So my own work has been sitting here well,

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:31.890
given that there's no vaccine for Malaria,

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:35.780
given that there's not much investments

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:37.080
in this negative diseases,

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:40.220
what can be done in the meantime?

00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:44.000
And they have some term like how do we say

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:45.580
coping strategies.

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:49.840
There are antimalarial bed
nets that you can sleep under

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:53.660
in order to protect
yourself and your children.

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:55.480
And so trying to understand
what are the barriers

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:59.320
to every household in
a malaria epidemic area

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:01.330
having access to that is
what I've been working on.

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:03.370
But over the years what
I've realized is that

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:05.850
there are so many of these issues

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:08.760
that poor households in poor
countries have to deal with.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:11.530
There's a bed net for Malaria.

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:14.030
There is making sure that your
water is not contaminated.

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:17.010
There's making sure that
you have enough iodine

00:13:17.010 --> 00:13:18.800
because your salt is not iodized

00:13:20.375 --> 00:13:21.950
and also stuff like that.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.770
And so the honors is really on the people

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:27.870
to take so many actions
to cope with the fact that

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:30.490
their environment is not safe.

00:13:30.490 --> 00:13:31.630
And to me, it's not sustainable.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:34.340
So going back to the definition
you just gave us, Catherine

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:37.170
that no one of the key
factor is safe environment.

00:13:37.170 --> 00:13:40.250
And to me, the biggest
source of health inequity

00:13:40.250 --> 00:13:43.630
in the world is really
between higher income

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:45.110
and lower income countries.

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:47.870
And it comes a lot from the difference

00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:49.762
in the safety and the environment.

00:13:49.762 --> 00:13:51.810
The fact that there are
some tropical diseases

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:54.900
or some issues with environment

00:13:56.410 --> 00:14:01.410
that that households
cannot subject to in a way,

00:14:01.412 --> 00:14:04.720
unless we try to tackle those
big environmental factors,

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.290
we are not gonna get equity in any way

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:08.750
if we think of it from the point of view

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.500
of across countries, not
just within the countries.

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:15.440
And so in as much as I'm
sympathetic to Sunil's complain

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:19.060
about top-down apertures
not being well-tailored,

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:22.100
I do think that for these
huge environmental concerns,

00:14:22.100 --> 00:14:23.660
we actually need top down,

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.070
because it's really about
coming up with the technologies,

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:27.960
coming up with vaccines,

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:29.690
getting the international
resources together

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:30.990
to get these vaccines out.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:35.140
And it's not something
that at the middle level

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:38.470
I don't have much as the poor country.

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:40.830
I don't have any leverage
to make that happen.

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:43.770
And so why the disease
or inefficient campaign

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:48.010
after World War II did not go as far as

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:49.670
one would have wished.

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:52.110
I'm gonna leave you on
this story Anne in the room

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:53.290
to speak to that.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:56.290
But I do think it's
important to try to go back

00:14:56.290 --> 00:15:01.290
to this and to really
not forget about this.

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:04.580
It originates in the
environment that people face.

00:15:05.485 --> 00:15:06.800
And within my own discipline economics,

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:10.230
I think over the last 10, 15, 20 years

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:13.030
has been kind of pushed
back against this sentiment

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:14.770
that geography matters.

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:16.080
It's all about institutions.

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:18.910
And just to bring your
geography back onto the table,

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:22.610
because I do think it's
a very important factor.

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:24.450
- Really, really helpful.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:29.160
And I wanna get back into our
conversation as we all jump in

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:34.143
about this debate over
geography institutions,

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:39.580
what are the structures
that lead to this inequity?

00:15:39.580 --> 00:15:41.670
We need to sort that
out before we figure out

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:43.180
how to tackle it.

00:15:43.180 --> 00:15:45.730
I also really appreciated
you talking about

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:48.610
really what this is
like at household level,

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:50.470
covering global health and
international development.

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:53.910
I write a lot about groups
working on X, Y or Z topic

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:54.890
related to health equity,

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:57.090
but from the perspective
of someone in a household,

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:59.010
it's a lot to take on
and it has to do with

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:00.870
the safety of their
environment, as you said.

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:02.730
So thank you for that.

00:16:02.730 --> 00:16:06.560
So Mesfin, I'd love for you to expand now,

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:08.720
kind of even further
on the human experience

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:12.380
of health inequities because
I know you deal with this

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:14.710
day in and day out and
health equity is very much

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:16.170
at the center of your work,

00:16:16.170 --> 00:16:18.040
the populations that you serve at IRC

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:20.590
often lack access to health services.

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:23.140
So, I wonder if you can
expand on the barriers

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:25.070
to access it come up in your work

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:27.470
and what that looks like
on an individual level.

00:16:28.581 --> 00:16:30.662
- Yeah. Thanks.

00:16:30.662 --> 00:16:31.495
Yeah, absolutely.

00:16:31.495 --> 00:16:35.560
I think that is one of
the challenge of all time

00:16:35.560 --> 00:16:37.923
in terms of equitable access to health.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:43.630
When I think of this I
always go back to the 2008

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:48.370
report by that of the commission on

00:16:48.370 --> 00:16:50.651
social determinant of health,

00:16:50.651 --> 00:16:53.700
which probably be one of the
most articulate document,

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:55.040
what are the condition

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:57.620
that actually create health inequity?

00:16:57.620 --> 00:16:59.830
And I think failure to address that

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:02.430
is what is leading us to
different health outcomes.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.410
And that's true for refugees,

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:06.900
that is true for people
in low income countries

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:09.203
or true for people in
high-income countries.

00:17:10.270 --> 00:17:14.690
So when it comes to refugees

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:18.710
and population affected by conflict,

00:17:18.710 --> 00:17:22.560
these are the people who
are the most vulnerable,

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.328
either directly impacted by disaster

00:17:25.328 --> 00:17:28.561
or running for their
life because of conflict.

00:17:28.561 --> 00:17:32.510
And even that is growing
because of climate change.

00:17:32.510 --> 00:17:37.510
And I think that is a
growing issue that we see

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:41.770
and it's was looking at the numbers,

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:43.890
number tells a story.

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:48.890
In 2021, nearly 235 million globally

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:50.850
get humanitarian aid.

00:17:50.850 --> 00:17:55.750
That is one in 33 people
of the global population.

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:58.860
So that is like before the pandemic,

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:00.630
that was like 172 million,

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:02.994
which was like a jumped by a 100 million.

00:18:02.994 --> 00:18:07.400
So this pandemic actually has hit hard

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:10.090
even those populations
who have the little means

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:14.640
to cope and as well as survive the impact

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:18.240
of pandemic or disease.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:23.240
So the barrier to access to
health has multiple factors.

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:29.010
And one is resource
despite the growing number

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:33.560
of refugees and internally
displaced maybe a fall

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:37.830
85% of them live in low
and middle income countries

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:40.570
who have also limited the
resources to provide healthcare

00:18:40.570 --> 00:18:42.100
for their whole population.

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:47.100
So that creates a huge strain
when you talk about refugees,

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:51.171
what comes on the media and
the headline is probably,

00:18:51.171 --> 00:18:55.820
refugee in the U.S or
perhaps maybe a few migration

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:59.610
in Europe who have the
means actually to handle it.

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:03.350
But not to forget that 85% of them

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:06.903
are hosted by countries like Uganda,

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:11.430
Ethiopia, or Pakistan.

00:19:11.430 --> 00:19:16.430
So, understanding the
need is a starting point.

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:19.810
Of course, the humanitarian system

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:23.010
has been generous in terms
of providing the resource,

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:26.960
but it has not been able to
match with the growing need.

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:30.135
Today, for population we need

00:19:30.135 --> 00:19:33.952
direct humanitarian assistance which is

00:19:33.952 --> 00:19:36.119
under the UN OCHA mandate,

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:42.880
only 36% of the needs are met
as of September, last month.

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:46.820
And which means that
people are basically living

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:49.940
in a situation where
there is no much resource

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:52.213
actually to provide the
care that they need.

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:58.030
And the other thing is these
places are characterized

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:01.520
by conflict in a way that
health facilities are damaged

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:02.710
or destroyed.

00:20:02.710 --> 00:20:04.153
And healthcare workers are killed.

00:20:06.626 --> 00:20:09.380
The safeguarding for health coalition

00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:13.230
who regularly monitor report in 2020

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:17.350
that attack on healthcare
system has grown.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:21.350
So in a way that when all these situations

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:25.400
are compounding and even the
little resource available

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:28.265
actually cannot be
accessed by the population.

00:20:28.265 --> 00:20:32.690
And that is the kind of situation
people are experiencing.

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:37.420
So perhaps maybe this reporting
might under count even

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:39.500
the really number of attacks
to healthcare workers

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:42.570
even much larger than
what's already reported.

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:47.570
And of course, the other issue
is that refugees by nature,

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:50.750
there are people who are
running for their lives.

00:20:50.750 --> 00:20:54.680
They are marginalized and
there is no political incentive

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:57.750
for countries that have
tried to provide protection

00:20:57.750 --> 00:21:02.750
unless they have expressed
policy to support

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:04.890
or host refugees.

00:21:04.890 --> 00:21:08.340
And also this politically divisive point.

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:12.190
You remember the Peacock
European refugee crisis

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:17.190
from Syria, a German
Chancellor plead to receive

00:21:17.810 --> 00:21:19.940
one million Syrian refugees.

00:21:19.940 --> 00:21:23.440
And we remember how
she received a backlash

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:25.192
for that commitment.

00:21:25.192 --> 00:21:28.340
So, really including refugees doesn't gain

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:30.090
you electoral votes.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:31.450
And as a result,

00:21:31.450 --> 00:21:34.910
these are people who
are being marginalized,

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:39.610
not necessarily included in
the national response plan.

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:41.980
And when it come to COVID today,

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:44.460
many national plans don't
include the refugees

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:46.310
in the COVID vaccination rollout.

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:48.750
That's why COVAX has
created what is called

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:51.340
humanitarian buffer stock,

00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:54.200
which is to meet those
population who may not be

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:57.100
otherwise included by
the national government.

00:21:57.100 --> 00:22:00.630
And I think, I can go into the policies

00:22:03.058 --> 00:22:04.580
and the number of people who ratified

00:22:04.580 --> 00:22:07.180
even the UN convention for refugee,

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:08.950
as well as the additional protocol,

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:11.080
which provides the legal
mandate for countries

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:13.657
to provide protection
and as well as a kind of

00:22:13.657 --> 00:22:16.010
assistance that refugees
can expect from government.

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:19.721
But not every country has a
signatory to those convention.

00:22:19.721 --> 00:22:22.660
As a result, the population are left

00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:26.050
at the mercy of humanitarian
assistance and kindness,

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:29.860
not necessarily that people
who have a right to claim.

00:22:29.860 --> 00:22:30.693
- Great points.

00:22:30.693 --> 00:22:31.600
Thank you so much.

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:33.610
I wanna pick up on one thing you said,

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:35.370
which you mentioned climate change,

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:37.000
and in terms of the urgency for action,

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.720
I'm glad you mentioned
that because that's gonna

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:42.623
only worsen these health equity challenges

00:22:42.623 --> 00:22:47.100
and really increase
that urgency for action.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:49.290
I wanna pick up on a theme that came up

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:50.910
throughout some of your remarks,

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:54.790
and that's whether we've
kind of talked about

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:57.060
what are these challenges when
it comes to health equity.

00:22:57.060 --> 00:22:58.790
In terms of the path forward,

00:22:58.790 --> 00:22:59.780
and as I said, at the outset,

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:03.200
the kinds of partnerships needed
to tackle these challenges.

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:04.980
One thing that that came
up a couple of times,

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:07.320
Sunil mentioned it,
Pascaline mentioned it,

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:11.040
is this idea of whether top-down works,

00:23:11.040 --> 00:23:12.853
whether top-down is needed,

00:23:13.758 --> 00:23:15.890
and there seems to be maybe
some disagreement on that,

00:23:15.890 --> 00:23:17.460
which I think is actually interesting

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:19.640
in terms of having a discussion here.

00:23:19.640 --> 00:23:21.428
And Mesfin, you just mentioned policy,

00:23:21.428 --> 00:23:24.270
policy being critical and
a challenge you're facing

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:26.665
when it comes to addressing health equity.

00:23:26.665 --> 00:23:27.840
So, let's talk about that for a moment

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:29.040
and a reminder to our audience,

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:30.760
keep those questions coming in on Q&amp;A,

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:32.340
because I certainly
have a lot of questions,

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:33.513
but I wanna hear yours.

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:36.120
But we'd love to just get your thoughts,

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:40.020
any reactions you have on how
top-down has or hasn't worked

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:43.650
in your work on whether
a historical perspective

00:23:43.650 --> 00:23:44.743
and economics perspective,

00:23:44.743 --> 00:23:46.230
a policy and practice perspective.

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:48.740
I think that'd be an interesting topic

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:50.707
to just hear thoughts on.

00:23:50.707 --> 00:23:53.223
So, Mesfin, I saw you
on mute, please jump in.

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:56.463
- Sure. Just in a couple
of points from me.

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.790
I mean, it depends on the
issue we are dealing of course,

00:24:00.790 --> 00:24:02.730
there are room for both top down

00:24:02.730 --> 00:24:04.190
as well as bottom up.

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:06.720
And I think, we need to be very clear

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:10.110
when we have a global
pandemic, like COVID,

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:13.000
we do need whole of society response.

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:17.733
And that includes communities,
national government,

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:21.500
as well as the global
system need to function,

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:23.380
including the UN system,

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:24.800
as well as a pharmaceutical

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:27.560
and all of the international system.

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:31.590
But unfortunately some of the system

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:34.570
are not efficient to respond to pandemic.

00:24:34.570 --> 00:24:37.120
If you look at access to technology,

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:41.020
which is a critical for vaccine

00:24:41.020 --> 00:24:44.320
and we have a system
that basically protect

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:47.650
access to technology through
the World Trade Organization,

00:24:47.650 --> 00:24:50.630
intellectual property related agreement,

00:24:50.630 --> 00:24:52.190
and it's not flexible.

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:56.107
And it also give undue
influence for people with

00:24:56.107 --> 00:24:57.700
high-income countries,

00:24:57.700 --> 00:24:58.910
not to make the system work

00:24:58.910 --> 00:25:02.580
for equitable access to technology.

00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:05.950
So, we should recognize
those inefficiencies

00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:08.810
in the system that may not be deploying

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:11.790
the kind of assistance we need to respond.

00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:13.690
But at the same time,

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:17.850
if you look at access
to vaccine and demand

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:22.450
that's where addressing misinformation,

00:25:22.450 --> 00:25:24.720
engaging communities from the outset

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:27.380
and creating really an
enabling environment

00:25:27.380 --> 00:25:31.660
where even the limited available
resource can be taken up.

00:25:31.660 --> 00:25:36.310
So I would think that we
need to pick our tools

00:25:36.310 --> 00:25:37.650
in the right way.

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:41.710
And I'm not in favor of one way
works better than the other,

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:45.230
but it depends on the situation
that we are dealing. Thanks.

00:25:45.230 --> 00:25:46.727
- Yeah, that makes a lot of sense.

00:25:46.727 --> 00:25:48.513
Go ahead, Sunil.

00:25:48.513 --> 00:25:50.670
- I think it was probably
unhelpful on my part

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:51.920
at the beginning to sort of frame it

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:53.950
as top down versus bottom up.

00:25:53.950 --> 00:25:58.370
That is often how this is
kind of framed as a problem.

00:25:58.370 --> 00:26:02.500
I think you know clearly
there are moments when,

00:26:02.500 --> 00:26:04.350
depending on the scale of the problem,

00:26:04.350 --> 00:26:05.970
and I think this does come down to scale.

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:08.510
There are different kinds of
challenges to health equity

00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:10.640
that operate on very,
very different scales.

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:13.100
And we probably need to
sort of deal with them well.

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:15.810
A global pandemic on
the one hand and perhaps

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:19.230
much more localized kinds
of challenges on the other.

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:22.823
I think one of the questions
historically speaking is,

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:28.270
it's undoubted that top-down
campaigns or interventions

00:26:28.270 --> 00:26:33.270
have very often had rapid
and effective results.

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:36.030
What we often find is that
those aren't sustained.

00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:39.120
And I think that's where
the kind of multilayered

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:40.780
or multi-level sort of intervention

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:42.380
that Dr. Mesfin is talking about,

00:26:42.380 --> 00:26:44.150
I think becomes very important.

00:26:44.150 --> 00:26:47.130
After the initial impact,
talk about the disease

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:50.040
eradication campaigns of the 1950s,

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:52.360
initially there were
tremendously successful,

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:54.840
particularly against malaria,

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:58.810
but then partly because of
the sort of vertical structure

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:01.180
of those campaigns there was very often

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:02.860
the sort of diminishing returns to that.

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:05.140
And then I think environmental challenges

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:06.463
also come into this.

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:12.010
I wanted to sort of jump
in and ask, Pascaline

00:27:12.010 --> 00:27:13.920
very interesting what
you were saying about

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:17.390
how geography matters and you keen

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:19.020
to sort of bring that back in.

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:21.050
I wonder if there are
insights from your work

00:27:21.050 --> 00:27:25.120
about how countries in the Global South

00:27:25.120 --> 00:27:29.900
which face similar
environmental conditions

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:31.940
then have very different trajectories

00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:33.570
when it comes to health outcomes.

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:36.820
I mean, have you found that
there are sort of particular

00:27:36.820 --> 00:27:38.650
so that it's not just the environment,

00:27:38.650 --> 00:27:40.200
if the environmental conditions

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:42.510
or the propensity to certain
kinds of tropical diseases

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:44.930
are shared, do you nevertheless see

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:48.610
significant divergence
between different countries

00:27:48.610 --> 00:27:51.570
as a result of the kinds
of policy interventions

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:54.493
that are made and which ones
are sort of effective there?

00:27:57.520 --> 00:27:59.590
- I know and I wanted to jump in earlier.

00:27:59.590 --> 00:28:01.040
So that's something we
should make sure that

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:02.113
to give us a mic.

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:08.020
Very quickly the dividends
that are comparing things

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:11.380
across countries is something that I think

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:13.911
we try to not do too
much because we know that

00:28:13.911 --> 00:28:16.540
there are so many things
that can come into play.

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:19.270
So what I can tell you for
sure that within a country,

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:21.140
there is a clear correlation in terms of

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:23.770
health outcomes and the environment

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:29.820
that people face depending on
the ability to fight disease.

00:28:30.915 --> 00:28:34.470
But of course, when you move
out of infectious diseases

00:28:34.470 --> 00:28:38.220
and move out of things that
are very much driven by

00:28:39.990 --> 00:28:41.731
the environment, there is an opportunity

00:28:41.731 --> 00:28:44.340
and there is opportunity
in the health system,

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:49.340
there's opportunity in
access to health insurance,

00:28:49.607 --> 00:28:51.030
in governance.

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:53.760
And that matters about for
whether the health workers

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:56.950
are gonna be well trained
and well motivated

00:28:56.950 --> 00:28:57.783
and present.

00:28:57.783 --> 00:29:00.710
So there's definitely a lot
of things that can be done

00:29:02.069 --> 00:29:04.293
on their side.

00:29:05.458 --> 00:29:08.550
And I think a lot of the
research has been focused on that

00:29:08.550 --> 00:29:11.910
because it's kind of like
easier to think about

00:29:11.910 --> 00:29:14.820
this approaches and to
work on these approaches

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:17.983
with individual governments,
new organizations,

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:21.350
because as Emanuelle said before,

00:29:21.350 --> 00:29:23.520
it's very context specific.

00:29:23.520 --> 00:29:25.157
So it's very hard to be
prescriptive and say,

00:29:25.157 --> 00:29:26.820
"This is how you're gonna improve

00:29:26.820 --> 00:29:28.110
or strengthen your health system.

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:29.510
It's really depends on the context."

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:34.250
And so doing this localized
work to make progress,

00:29:34.250 --> 00:29:36.840
I think is it's been at least in my field

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:39.690
people are, "So this is possible.

00:29:39.690 --> 00:29:40.747
So let's do that."

00:29:41.950 --> 00:29:44.820
I just wanna say, there's a ceiling

00:29:44.820 --> 00:29:47.080
to where we can go with that.

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:51.573
And on the larger environmental
stuff we need global effort.

00:29:53.690 --> 00:29:54.870
- Just building on that for a second,

00:29:54.870 --> 00:29:57.500
I mentioned earlier the Robert
Wood Johnson Foundation,

00:29:57.500 --> 00:30:01.140
one interesting role I've
seen philanthropy play

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:04.660
is not just directly funding
work to address health equity,

00:30:04.660 --> 00:30:08.150
but trying to highlight what
are some of those exemplars,

00:30:08.150 --> 00:30:09.830
kind of building on Sunil's question

00:30:09.830 --> 00:30:12.590
like despite some similar challenges

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:14.900
that different geographies might face,

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:16.730
there have been better
outcomes in this place,

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:17.563
and why is that?

00:30:17.563 --> 00:30:22.563
And funding research on why
and sharing that research

00:30:22.880 --> 00:30:24.950
to inform work in other contexts.

00:30:24.950 --> 00:30:28.030
But, as Anne-Emanuelle, said earlier

00:30:28.030 --> 00:30:30.060
contexts are quite different
and context matters.

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:32.050
So, I do wanna bring you in though,

00:30:32.050 --> 00:30:33.830
'cause I know you had a
comment to make earlier.

00:30:33.830 --> 00:30:34.663
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:30:34.663 --> 00:30:36.540
It's not just that context matters,

00:30:36.540 --> 00:30:38.790
it's politics and power that matter.

00:30:38.790 --> 00:30:42.570
And I think those elements
have been under-emphasized

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:44.590
in the conversation this far.

00:30:44.590 --> 00:30:47.500
Even if we're looking at
the difference between

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:51.120
healthcare equity and health equity,

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:55.393
we see this playing out
in very, very patent ways.

00:30:56.548 --> 00:30:59.770
And so important as it is looking at

00:30:59.770 --> 00:31:02.130
the symptoms or the end game,

00:31:02.130 --> 00:31:05.260
the horrific situation of refugees

00:31:05.260 --> 00:31:07.890
or so-called neglected diseases.

00:31:07.890 --> 00:31:10.250
What we should be doing is asking

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:13.540
who is doing the neglecting, why and how?

00:31:13.540 --> 00:31:16.770
After all trypanosomiasis
was not neglected

00:31:16.770 --> 00:31:19.470
in the late 19th and early 20th century

00:31:19.470 --> 00:31:23.000
by German and British
and other colonial powers

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:25.470
rather it was a huge threat

00:31:25.470 --> 00:31:29.370
to territorial expansion
and acquisition, right?

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:34.370
So how and why certain health
conditions become more or less

00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:38.830
important under particular
contexts at the global level,

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:43.780
and then where politics both
local and global play out.

00:31:43.780 --> 00:31:47.580
And Sunil mentioned at the
outset of change in the 1970s,

00:31:47.580 --> 00:31:49.620
but really this change was coming,

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:52.510
starting with decolonization in the 1950s

00:31:52.510 --> 00:31:56.400
the BANJAN Conference and
a non-aligned movement

00:31:56.400 --> 00:31:58.810
and the largest, the G-77,

00:31:58.810 --> 00:32:02.570
the largest political grouping
within the United nations

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:06.310
that was able to push forward the concept

00:32:06.310 --> 00:32:07.770
and ideally the approach

00:32:07.770 --> 00:32:10.440
of a new international economic order.

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:13.520
So, how is it and why is it

00:32:13.520 --> 00:32:16.820
that power is distributed so unequally?

00:32:16.820 --> 00:32:19.630
So yes, the trips agreement and adherence

00:32:19.630 --> 00:32:24.260
toward a huge issue in terms
of vaccine access, right?

00:32:24.260 --> 00:32:27.130
But then the question is
how is it that the WTO

00:32:27.130 --> 00:32:31.370
gets set up under whose influence, right?

00:32:31.370 --> 00:32:34.230
And why are the rules
of the game so uneven

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:38.590
and so unfair in terms
of Europe has plenty

00:32:38.590 --> 00:32:42.890
and North America have plenty
of subsidies for agriculture

00:32:42.890 --> 00:32:46.090
and this has prevented
for Global South countries

00:32:46.090 --> 00:32:49.400
who are not permitted to
have tariffs and so on.

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:51.090
So I think what we need to do

00:32:51.090 --> 00:32:55.620
getting back to this health
and healthcare equity situation

00:32:55.620 --> 00:32:59.930
is think through what the
structural determinants

00:32:59.930 --> 00:33:04.930
or societal determination
of health as (indistinct)

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:08.340
and other Latin American social
medicine scholars put it.

00:33:08.340 --> 00:33:12.480
And thinks through not
just what the factors are,

00:33:12.480 --> 00:33:15.470
but what are the processes
and the rules of the game

00:33:15.470 --> 00:33:18.093
and the power wielding that takes place.

00:33:19.180 --> 00:33:22.730
The refugee situation at
bottom needs to be addressed

00:33:22.730 --> 00:33:24.920
by addressing militarism, right?

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:28.880
And the fact that the
five permanent members

00:33:28.880 --> 00:33:32.660
of the security council are
the largest arms exporters'

00:33:32.660 --> 00:33:33.500
in the world.

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:37.580
It is a massive problem
that needs priority now.

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:38.836
You could say,

00:33:38.836 --> 00:33:41.370
"Well, you've got a
situation on the ground,

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:44.160
how can you deal quickly
with structural issues?"

00:33:44.160 --> 00:33:48.183
But we've been saying this for
decades and decades, right?

00:33:49.052 --> 00:33:51.810
So, if not now when, right?

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:56.810
It is time and the fact
that so many have suffered

00:33:57.900 --> 00:34:00.500
in such a great extent, and of course,

00:34:00.500 --> 00:34:05.500
far greater extent from
COVID-19 in countries

00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:09.420
with a huge asymmetries economic, social,

00:34:09.420 --> 00:34:13.470
political asymmetries is a wake up call,

00:34:13.470 --> 00:34:15.250
but certainly nothing new.

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:18.920
And so, I think it
behooves us all who work

00:34:18.920 --> 00:34:22.510
in the larger global development field

00:34:22.510 --> 00:34:26.275
really to be constantly
pushing at all ends.

00:34:26.275 --> 00:34:29.700
And certainly on these structural factors

00:34:29.700 --> 00:34:33.473
less they be placed in the
background as they typically are.

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:35.210
- I totally agree.

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:38.390
So let's bring those structural
factors to the foreground,

00:34:38.390 --> 00:34:41.543
any reactions to what
Anna-Emanuelle just said.

00:34:43.197 --> 00:34:44.610
We've had these conversations before,

00:34:44.610 --> 00:34:47.010
but is the conversation
any different this time

00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:50.000
in terms of the power and politics at play

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:52.070
when it comes to health and equity,

00:34:52.070 --> 00:34:53.223
healthcare and equity?

00:34:56.119 --> 00:34:57.300
You might wanna jump in.

00:34:57.300 --> 00:34:58.300
Go ahead, Pascaline.

00:35:00.360 --> 00:35:04.120
- So, I think deep down I
agree with Anne-Emanuelle

00:35:04.120 --> 00:35:08.450
but at the same time it
makes me to give this comment

00:35:10.147 --> 00:35:12.940
as pessimistic when I
think about the fact that,

00:35:12.940 --> 00:35:14.710
okay, we are not gonna make any progress

00:35:14.710 --> 00:35:18.220
if we don't change all
this structural problem,

00:35:18.220 --> 00:35:23.020
I feel like it's gonna
have even one extra layer

00:35:25.942 --> 00:35:28.330
of I already have a saying,

00:35:28.330 --> 00:35:30.550
it's hard to think about getting agreement

00:35:30.550 --> 00:35:31.893
to fix the environment.

00:35:33.460 --> 00:35:37.860
If on top of that, we have
to bring in the monetary.

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:42.860
I just feel like people
say it's not happening

00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:44.730
and there's just give up.

00:35:46.762 --> 00:35:51.762
So, I wanna find room for
progress in a very short run,

00:35:53.810 --> 00:35:54.650
but then there's always this tension,

00:35:54.650 --> 00:35:56.650
like how do we make
progress in the short run

00:35:56.650 --> 00:36:01.650
without forgetting to also
deal with these deep issues

00:36:02.802 --> 00:36:04.265
that are so important.

00:36:04.265 --> 00:36:06.480
I hear you, Anne-Emanuelle
I just feel like

00:36:07.596 --> 00:36:12.596
do you see any hope for
a change on those fronts?

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:17.710
- Mesfin, you wanna jump in?

00:36:17.710 --> 00:36:19.080
- Yeah, sure.

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:20.460
This make couple of thoughts.

00:36:20.460 --> 00:36:22.860
I mean, when you look at the problem,

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:26.140
I think Anne-Emanuelle
has came to the picture.

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:31.000
You may feel like we are
paralyzed, what can you do?

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:35.560
I do believe there are
effort to address this,

00:36:35.560 --> 00:36:38.410
when it comes to developmental in equity,

00:36:38.410 --> 00:36:40.930
I mean, we have seen, for example,

00:36:40.930 --> 00:36:43.790
how the MGD helps to
address child mortality

00:36:43.790 --> 00:36:45.120
and maternal mortality.

00:36:45.120 --> 00:36:48.420
And we'll move to the SDG with a mantra

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:50.193
of leaving no one behind.

00:36:52.070 --> 00:36:54.730
That was tested during the pandemic,

00:36:54.730 --> 00:36:57.610
actually what we mean
leaving nobody behind

00:36:57.610 --> 00:36:59.603
when you are rolling out the vaccine

00:36:59.603 --> 00:37:01.410
actually we have left everybody behind.

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:05.670
So in a way, I think we have to translate

00:37:05.670 --> 00:37:09.980
this high level of rhetoric
into actionable plan

00:37:09.980 --> 00:37:11.650
and implementation.

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:13.500
And there are a couple of efforts

00:37:13.500 --> 00:37:15.760
to bring the humanitarian development,

00:37:15.760 --> 00:37:17.820
peace building initiative
through what's called

00:37:17.820 --> 00:37:21.722
a Humanitarian-Development-Piece-Nexus,

00:37:21.722 --> 00:37:25.810
recognizing that these are not dichotomy

00:37:25.810 --> 00:37:27.340
that exist in parallel

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:28.950
it's actually the same countries

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:31.490
that are going through
different level of fragility

00:37:31.490 --> 00:37:33.920
and also countries affected by conflict

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:36.320
and also the same countries where we think

00:37:37.690 --> 00:37:39.250
we should be doing development aid.

00:37:39.250 --> 00:37:42.370
So in a way, how do you
bring the actor together

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:44.640
in a way that we can
address the root causes?

00:37:44.640 --> 00:37:47.130
Not only just sending humanitarian aid

00:37:47.130 --> 00:37:49.810
when people are starting to move.

00:37:49.810 --> 00:37:54.050
So, and again, when it comes to health,

00:37:54.050 --> 00:37:57.930
the pandemic has told us
that we need to invest

00:37:57.930 --> 00:37:59.890
in public health infrastructure

00:37:59.890 --> 00:38:04.680
and public health infrastructure
is not something that is

00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:08.320
appealing to private sector.

00:38:08.320 --> 00:38:10.710
And this is where
government needs to invest

00:38:11.570 --> 00:38:13.520
on disease surveillance.

00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:16.040
Probably that's not
what the private sector

00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:18.242
would be interested to invest.

00:38:18.242 --> 00:38:21.750
So in a way I think, see trading where

00:38:21.750 --> 00:38:23.600
government has a role to play.

00:38:23.600 --> 00:38:27.950
I think that role has to be
enforced through accountability

00:38:27.950 --> 00:38:31.670
and also we need to measure and
show where there is progress

00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:35.110
so that people can see what's
working, what's not working.

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:39.285
And translating this broad mantra

00:38:39.285 --> 00:38:41.670
about how this health coverage

00:38:41.670 --> 00:38:43.760
or leaving no one behind.

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:45.980
What does it mean in practical sense

00:38:45.980 --> 00:38:48.980
when you are doing either vaccination

00:38:48.980 --> 00:38:52.760
or when you are dealing with disease

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:55.960
or any of the health
issues that we are dealing.

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:59.710
So, I do believe that there is a way,

00:38:59.710 --> 00:39:02.770
but I think what is
missing in many ways is

00:39:02.770 --> 00:39:04.393
lack of political wheel.

00:39:05.717 --> 00:39:09.200
And of course, unless you
have a democratic system

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:14.060
where electorate can
hold leaders accountable,

00:39:14.060 --> 00:39:16.940
unfortunately, that's a rhetoric

00:39:16.940 --> 00:39:19.010
which may not be necessarily translated,

00:39:19.010 --> 00:39:22.950
but I think we need to measure and show

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:25.030
where this is happening and probably

00:39:26.153 --> 00:39:28.153
from the countries who are doing better.

00:39:30.280 --> 00:39:31.113
- Absolutely.

00:39:31.113 --> 00:39:31.946
Go ahead.

00:39:31.946 --> 00:39:32.870
- I was just going to say,

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:36.470
the idea is not to feel despondent.

00:39:36.470 --> 00:39:38.220
In fact, it's an enormous privilege

00:39:38.220 --> 00:39:40.750
to be able to feel despondent
as opposed to needing

00:39:40.750 --> 00:39:44.140
to take action day to day.

00:39:44.140 --> 00:39:47.790
I think what's crucial is understanding

00:39:47.790 --> 00:39:52.790
how the global order, the global
political economy operates.

00:39:53.560 --> 00:39:57.590
And then think through,
as Mesfin was suggesting,

00:39:57.590 --> 00:39:59.750
what are some of the leavers, right?

00:39:59.750 --> 00:40:02.708
And perhaps some of the best leavers are

00:40:02.708 --> 00:40:05.610
why not a campaign to ultimately put

00:40:05.610 --> 00:40:08.190
humanitarian organizations
out of business,

00:40:08.190 --> 00:40:10.930
all due respect, so
that we don't need them

00:40:10.930 --> 00:40:12.540
in a few years, right?

00:40:12.540 --> 00:40:17.540
So that we have a system where
it's safe to live in Sudan,

00:40:18.850 --> 00:40:23.850
and there's a redistribution
of income and resources

00:40:23.950 --> 00:40:27.380
so that everyone has a decent housing

00:40:27.380 --> 00:40:32.063
and decent education and
piped water and paved roads,

00:40:33.380 --> 00:40:36.620
and full employment and
good employment, right?

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:41.210
So that we don't have these
hugely inequitable effects

00:40:41.210 --> 00:40:45.300
and impacts on the most
oppressed groups of people.

00:40:45.300 --> 00:40:50.300
So, I do think we need
to use our own positions

00:40:51.660 --> 00:40:56.650
of privilege to speak out and
to point to these connections

00:40:56.650 --> 00:41:00.110
and to go beyond, you
mentioned partnerships,

00:41:00.110 --> 00:41:03.010
I really worry about
public private partnerships

00:41:03.010 --> 00:41:05.580
because the private sector and certainly

00:41:05.580 --> 00:41:08.890
the corporate sector in the
business of maximizing profits,

00:41:08.890 --> 00:41:12.950
not in the business of
improving social conditions.

00:41:12.950 --> 00:41:16.680
So, I think one needs
to be quite cautious.

00:41:16.680 --> 00:41:19.830
And in that and in regard of partnerships,

00:41:19.830 --> 00:41:22.750
which unfortunately the
STGs are emphasizing

00:41:22.750 --> 00:41:27.750
and also seek to support
true democratic movements

00:41:30.410 --> 00:41:33.080
and efforts and there's
been so much intervention

00:41:33.080 --> 00:41:36.460
certainly in the Cold War,
but in other time periods,

00:41:36.460 --> 00:41:41.460
when people's uprising for a new form

00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:44.810
of accountable government,

00:41:44.810 --> 00:41:49.220
and the U.S or other forces have come in

00:41:49.220 --> 00:41:54.220
and nipped those efforts often
very violently in the bud.

00:41:55.210 --> 00:42:00.210
So, again, big issues, but
not ones that we can ignore.

00:42:00.710 --> 00:42:01.543
- Absolutely.

00:42:01.543 --> 00:42:03.100
And I think this is
resonating with the audience.

00:42:03.100 --> 00:42:05.790
I'm gonna share a comment
from the audience,

00:42:05.790 --> 00:42:07.400
and maybe we can kind of gear it

00:42:07.400 --> 00:42:10.027
into the form of a question,
but this attendee says,

00:42:10.027 --> 00:42:11.990
"Thank you, Anne-Emannuelle for addressing

00:42:11.990 --> 00:42:14.360
the power dynamics that influence health.

00:42:14.360 --> 00:42:17.070
I'm very interested in the
intersection of health equity

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:20.660
and economics equity can not
be achieved until root causes,

00:42:20.660 --> 00:42:22.910
colonialism racism,
unfair trade practices,

00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:24.320
et cetera are addressed."

00:42:24.320 --> 00:42:26.350
So that's just a comment
to give you a sense

00:42:26.350 --> 00:42:28.920
of where at least one
member of our audience

00:42:28.920 --> 00:42:31.700
is standing and seeing these issues.

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:33.570
I have some specific
questions I wanna dive into

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:34.960
and I wanna bring in
more from the audience,

00:42:34.960 --> 00:42:37.110
but I wanna give space for any panelists

00:42:37.110 --> 00:42:38.830
to jump with reactions, Sunil.

00:42:38.830 --> 00:42:40.410
- Yeah. If I might just pick up

00:42:40.410 --> 00:42:42.070
to develop the conversation

00:42:42.070 --> 00:42:43.980
and including that interesting comment

00:42:43.980 --> 00:42:45.920
from our audience member.

00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:48.320
I'm gonna pick up on
something, Mesfin said,

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:52.190
which was the question of political will

00:42:52.190 --> 00:42:57.190
and particularly the question
of what political means

00:42:57.730 --> 00:43:00.380
in different kinds of political system.

00:43:00.380 --> 00:43:02.670
One of the things that's always puzzled,

00:43:02.670 --> 00:43:06.330
I think many of us who work
on health in South Asia

00:43:06.330 --> 00:43:11.330
is how rarely health or health equity

00:43:12.640 --> 00:43:15.460
have been major political
issues, even in India,

00:43:15.460 --> 00:43:20.460
even in the context of regular
democratic competition,

00:43:22.630 --> 00:43:24.060
things might be changing in India,

00:43:24.060 --> 00:43:25.860
but let's bracket that for a minute.

00:43:27.660 --> 00:43:28.580
Some parts of India,

00:43:28.580 --> 00:43:30.510
this is also profoundly context specific,

00:43:30.510 --> 00:43:33.310
there some parts of
India where governments

00:43:33.310 --> 00:43:36.470
perhaps do fall over questions of health.

00:43:36.470 --> 00:43:38.780
But by and large, that
has not been the case

00:43:38.780 --> 00:43:41.430
that never been the case in
India since independence.

00:43:42.995 --> 00:43:44.590
I'm not sure I have an answer to why

00:43:44.590 --> 00:43:46.350
I've been thinking
about this for 20 years.

00:43:46.350 --> 00:43:49.200
And I'm not really sure what it is

00:43:49.200 --> 00:43:50.928
whereby in certain societies.

00:43:50.928 --> 00:43:53.650
And I think Anne-Emanuelle,
your Latin American examples

00:43:53.650 --> 00:43:55.310
are probably better ones there.

00:43:55.310 --> 00:43:59.480
There has been mass
mobilization for health

00:44:00.810 --> 00:44:05.810
coming from kind of upsurge
of democratic demand.

00:44:06.000 --> 00:44:07.880
And there are places where
that hasn't happened.

00:44:07.880 --> 00:44:09.460
And I just wanted to
put that on the table,

00:44:09.460 --> 00:44:11.170
'cause I don't have answers to it,

00:44:11.170 --> 00:44:13.350
but it is something that
has often puzzled me

00:44:13.350 --> 00:44:15.080
must have been mentioned political will.

00:44:15.080 --> 00:44:18.070
I think that's true, not just at the level

00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:20.240
of the permanent members, current accounts

00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:22.004
and the global governments,

00:44:22.004 --> 00:44:26.370
but it also in a sense that
continues all the way down

00:44:26.370 --> 00:44:27.980
even to the level of local government.

00:44:27.980 --> 00:44:31.850
Where is it and when is
it that health equity

00:44:31.850 --> 00:44:34.770
to use just the topic
of our discussion today

00:44:35.680 --> 00:44:37.763
become something people fight for.

00:44:39.770 --> 00:44:41.760
- Anne-Emanuelle, do you
have a reaction to that?

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:44.550
- Well, I was just gonna
say I think in South Asia,

00:44:44.550 --> 00:44:48.060
they're the classic examples of Kerala,

00:44:48.060 --> 00:44:52.090
the state of Kerala in
Southern India and Sri Lanka.

00:44:52.090 --> 00:44:54.820
And so in the case of Sri Lanka,

00:44:54.820 --> 00:44:58.280
a free universal, health
free the point of service,

00:44:58.280 --> 00:45:02.410
universal healthcare system
that developed quite quickly

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:06.020
in the post-colonial
period and was fought for

00:45:06.020 --> 00:45:08.977
and struggled for and exists

00:45:08.977 --> 00:45:11.790
to a certain extent to this day.

00:45:11.790 --> 00:45:16.130
I mean, it's being ratcheted
down through another force

00:45:16.130 --> 00:45:17.580
that we haven't discussed,

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:21.020
which is the neoliberal
phase of global capitalism.

00:45:21.020 --> 00:45:25.100
But, in the case of
Kerala it's so interesting

00:45:26.070 --> 00:45:28.020
at times there've been
communist governments,

00:45:28.020 --> 00:45:29.310
at times, not,

00:45:29.310 --> 00:45:34.310
but the public support for
universal public health care

00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:41.420
and education and housing
supports and so on is so strong

00:45:41.420 --> 00:45:44.320
that even when the communists
are elected out of office

00:45:44.320 --> 00:45:47.030
and another party is in power,

00:45:47.030 --> 00:45:49.280
those issues remain on the table.

00:45:49.280 --> 00:45:54.240
So, political will doesn't
simply come from politicians,

00:45:54.240 --> 00:45:56.480
but it comes from those who are holding

00:45:56.480 --> 00:46:00.150
as was said by Mesfin holding
politicians accountable

00:46:00.150 --> 00:46:03.280
and also seeing what's possible, right?

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:07.760
Once that's tasted, then
the demands also rise

00:46:07.760 --> 00:46:08.790
to a great extent.

00:46:08.790 --> 00:46:11.470
I mean, I think we're
seeing that in Brazil

00:46:11.470 --> 00:46:16.040
with the uprising against
Bolsonaro went in prior decades,

00:46:16.040 --> 00:46:18.630
there's this creation of a fantastic,

00:46:18.630 --> 00:46:21.350
a single healthcare system

00:46:21.350 --> 00:46:25.240
and enormous social
investments and family wages

00:46:25.240 --> 00:46:30.070
and so on that would
enable 40 million people

00:46:30.070 --> 00:46:32.800
to be lifted out of poverty and so on.

00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:36.860
And so people are reacting against that

00:46:36.860 --> 00:46:38.010
and people in the streets.

00:46:38.010 --> 00:46:43.010
So I do think it happens and
that including in South Asia,

00:46:43.150 --> 00:46:46.640
not withstanding repressive
governments at times

00:46:46.640 --> 00:46:49.770
and including now in India.

00:46:49.770 --> 00:46:53.130
- I think you're absolutely
right, Anne-Emanuelle.

00:46:53.130 --> 00:46:55.270
The point is more that Kerala in Sri Lanka

00:46:55.270 --> 00:46:56.920
has such outliers in South Asia.

00:46:56.920 --> 00:46:59.780
The question is why is it happening there?

00:46:59.780 --> 00:47:01.700
To some extent, but nevertheless,

00:47:01.700 --> 00:47:05.130
I mean, I think that's why
they're so interesting I think,

00:47:05.130 --> 00:47:07.370
and there has been so much
sort of study what it is

00:47:07.370 --> 00:47:09.820
about those two particular contexts.

00:47:09.820 --> 00:47:12.930
And I think there are many
other examples around the world,

00:47:12.930 --> 00:47:16.240
which I take optimism to some extent

00:47:16.240 --> 00:47:19.000
that there have been repeated
sort of mobilizations

00:47:19.000 --> 00:47:21.770
to hold the powers that be accountable

00:47:21.770 --> 00:47:24.190
in relation to health.

00:47:24.190 --> 00:47:26.020
- I wanna bring in a question

00:47:26.020 --> 00:47:28.420
and this is something I'm
personally very interested in.

00:47:28.420 --> 00:47:30.310
So hopefully audiences is as well.

00:47:30.310 --> 00:47:33.470
But when we talk about leavers
for short-term progress,

00:47:33.470 --> 00:47:36.870
even as we are addressing
these larger structural issues

00:47:36.870 --> 00:47:37.953
that need addressing.

00:47:39.540 --> 00:47:43.120
I cover innovation and
technology quite a bit,

00:47:43.120 --> 00:47:46.140
and at least perhaps this
is an over-hyped space,

00:47:46.140 --> 00:47:48.720
but digital is often
brought up as something that

00:47:48.720 --> 00:47:50.463
could advance health equity.

00:47:51.880 --> 00:47:54.680
It's also possibly
something that can worsen

00:47:54.680 --> 00:47:57.180
health inequities because
not everyone for example

00:47:57.180 --> 00:47:59.679
has access to broadband or smartphones.

00:47:59.679 --> 00:48:02.610
But I did just wanna bring
up the role of digital.

00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:04.690
I'm curious if the panelists
have reactions to that.

00:48:04.690 --> 00:48:06.410
I know Anne-Emanuelle, for example,

00:48:06.410 --> 00:48:08.530
you did some work on
whether digital technology

00:48:08.530 --> 00:48:10.133
can address health inequities,

00:48:11.928 --> 00:48:12.870
within and across countries.

00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:15.300
And you concluded that
it can be useful tool,

00:48:15.300 --> 00:48:16.810
but only under certain conditions.

00:48:16.810 --> 00:48:19.530
So I wonder if you can just quickly expand

00:48:19.530 --> 00:48:21.210
on what are those conditions,

00:48:21.210 --> 00:48:23.270
and then I'd love to hear if
other panelists have reactions

00:48:23.270 --> 00:48:25.020
on how that's come up in your work.

00:48:27.550 --> 00:48:28.413
- Sure.

00:48:29.340 --> 00:48:33.680
Well, you can't consider technology

00:48:33.680 --> 00:48:37.460
divorced from social and
political conditions.

00:48:37.460 --> 00:48:41.460
So it's not just the
(indistinct) drops down.

00:48:41.460 --> 00:48:43.263
I mean, even if you had,

00:48:44.264 --> 00:48:46.580
well we have a supposed
malaria vaccine now,

00:48:46.580 --> 00:48:49.320
although it's really an
immune booster, but anyway,

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:50.470
we'll leave that aside.

00:48:51.750 --> 00:48:53.830
Even if you had that, right?

00:48:53.830 --> 00:48:58.080
This quick fix, what would happen then

00:48:58.080 --> 00:49:02.500
to all of the efforts to
improve housing stock, right?

00:49:02.500 --> 00:49:05.830
So that would end up
generating further inequities.

00:49:05.830 --> 00:49:08.360
And then of course, who would be reached,

00:49:08.360 --> 00:49:10.630
who wouldn't be reached and so on.

00:49:10.630 --> 00:49:13.010
So I also think it's crucial,

00:49:13.010 --> 00:49:16.070
especially with current day technology

00:49:16.070 --> 00:49:19.340
to think through every step
of the commodity change.

00:49:19.340 --> 00:49:24.027
So we are using digital devices constantly

00:49:25.000 --> 00:49:30.000
without taking into account the thousands

00:49:30.140 --> 00:49:32.370
of child artisanal miners

00:49:32.370 --> 00:49:34.880
in the Democratic Republic of Congo

00:49:34.880 --> 00:49:37.900
who were abused, mistreated,

00:49:37.900 --> 00:49:40.830
exposed to cobalt in the mining process,

00:49:40.830 --> 00:49:41.890
et cetera, et cetera,

00:49:41.890 --> 00:49:44.540
not to mention every other
step in the commodity chain.

00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:47.730
So we tend to kind of put technology

00:49:47.730 --> 00:49:50.360
in this very decontextualized box

00:49:50.360 --> 00:49:53.683
and, yes, we certainly
knew our interactions

00:49:53.683 --> 00:49:56.640
are all shaped by technology,

00:49:56.640 --> 00:50:01.640
but technology is also shaped
by the very power structures

00:50:01.660 --> 00:50:03.490
that we've been talking about today.

00:50:03.490 --> 00:50:06.310
I mean, who owns technological companies?

00:50:06.310 --> 00:50:09.310
What's the role of metal mining?

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:12.010
I come from a country, Canada,

00:50:12.010 --> 00:50:16.730
that has a heinous record human
rights and ill health record

00:50:16.730 --> 00:50:21.620
in terms of its mining
involvement in Latin America,

00:50:21.620 --> 00:50:24.150
it's extractive industries
in Latin America.

00:50:24.150 --> 00:50:27.230
So I think it's really morally

00:50:27.230 --> 00:50:31.670
and really analytically
bereft to not consider

00:50:31.670 --> 00:50:34.787
the role of technology, to
talk about digital technology

00:50:34.787 --> 00:50:39.620
and equity without considering
all of these implications.

00:50:39.620 --> 00:50:43.070
So, it could be a useful tool,

00:50:43.070 --> 00:50:45.410
but when it's embedded in policies

00:50:45.410 --> 00:50:48.150
that redistribute power and resources

00:50:48.150 --> 00:50:50.180
and do not concentrate harms,

00:50:50.180 --> 00:50:52.693
which technology absolutely does.

00:50:53.590 --> 00:50:55.110
- Good points on technology things.

00:50:55.110 --> 00:50:56.590
I should definitely keep
in mind in my reporting.

00:50:56.590 --> 00:50:57.870
I love your point on not putting it

00:50:57.870 --> 00:50:59.440
in a decontextualized box.

00:50:59.440 --> 00:51:00.950
So I don't mean to do that,

00:51:00.950 --> 00:51:03.750
but any other reactions
to the role it might play

00:51:03.750 --> 00:51:07.380
while considering that
complexity go ahead, Mesfin.

00:51:07.380 --> 00:51:11.690
- Yeah, I think technology
has a huge problem

00:51:11.690 --> 00:51:14.550
is in terms of addressing health inequity,

00:51:14.550 --> 00:51:18.770
especially in Southern
Sub-Saharan Africa, for example,

00:51:18.770 --> 00:51:21.940
is a place I know well
that how much actually

00:51:21.940 --> 00:51:25.620
mobile technology has thrust
movement in people's life,

00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:27.690
whether it's a mobile
payment, for example,

00:51:27.690 --> 00:51:29.900
access to cash for people who are

00:51:29.900 --> 00:51:31.410
not traditionally connected

00:51:31.410 --> 00:51:33.323
to the mainstream banking system.

00:51:34.250 --> 00:51:37.960
So, access information of course,

00:51:37.960 --> 00:51:39.920
it has to be regulated.

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:42.820
It has to be in an onwards
that's understandable

00:51:42.820 --> 00:51:45.883
by the local community.

00:51:49.539 --> 00:51:51.460
And there should be some
level of digital literacy

00:51:51.460 --> 00:51:53.400
as well that go with it.

00:51:53.400 --> 00:51:56.010
It can be harmful as it is useful it is.

00:51:56.010 --> 00:51:59.840
And we have seen how
misinformation kind of spread

00:51:59.840 --> 00:52:04.270
very fast through social media
and all of those networks,

00:52:04.270 --> 00:52:06.060
but at the same time, the same platform

00:52:06.060 --> 00:52:09.460
can be a cause for good cause.

00:52:09.460 --> 00:52:14.460
So, I mean, it has a lot of potential

00:52:14.710 --> 00:52:16.263
and in humanitarian settings,

00:52:17.130 --> 00:52:19.680
this is one of the area
where access to information

00:52:19.680 --> 00:52:23.240
is actually as vital as
the life saving as it is

00:52:23.240 --> 00:52:24.240
like access to food.

00:52:26.090 --> 00:52:29.800
I recall during the Syria
crisis earlier days,

00:52:29.800 --> 00:52:33.340
even for people where to seek asylum

00:52:34.360 --> 00:52:36.845
and knowing their legal rights

00:52:36.845 --> 00:52:41.010
and what is the safest place
to be and for children.

00:52:41.010 --> 00:52:44.610
So all these kinds of emotions
are lifesaving for people

00:52:44.610 --> 00:52:47.450
when they're on the move.

00:52:47.450 --> 00:52:52.450
So I do think technology
has enormous potential,

00:52:52.730 --> 00:52:57.480
but as Anne-Emanuelle said
it has to be regulated

00:52:57.480 --> 00:53:02.480
in a way that is used and
it can also cause harm

00:53:03.130 --> 00:53:05.310
if it is not proper to use or utilize.

00:53:05.310 --> 00:53:08.312
So, in the place where I work

00:53:08.312 --> 00:53:12.210
it's taking off addressing
many of the issue

00:53:12.210 --> 00:53:14.163
that is related to health inequity.

00:53:17.160 --> 00:53:18.160
- Other thoughts on technology?

00:53:18.160 --> 00:53:19.390
Otherwise I certainly
have other questions.

00:53:19.390 --> 00:53:20.820
Go ahead, Pascaline.

00:53:20.820 --> 00:53:25.407
- I just want to add one
area where it can be useful,

00:53:27.180 --> 00:53:31.563
which is, did you see
any early warning system

00:53:31.563 --> 00:53:34.262
and actually knowing when
there is an epidemic starting

00:53:34.262 --> 00:53:38.523
like Ebola even COVID
has been extremely useful

00:53:38.523 --> 00:53:41.490
to be able to kind of have a sense of like

00:53:41.490 --> 00:53:45.740
where there is more or
less people being infected.

00:53:45.740 --> 00:53:48.320
And technology can
definitely help with that

00:53:48.320 --> 00:53:49.673
with obviously all the drawbacks

00:53:49.673 --> 00:53:54.220
that that means also monitoring people,

00:53:54.220 --> 00:53:57.440
and there's like the big brother civilians

00:53:57.440 --> 00:53:59.130
is a term that can be interpreted

00:54:03.451 --> 00:54:06.069
very negatively or very
positively, whether or not

00:54:06.069 --> 00:54:08.840
you're just surveilling people
or surveilling the visitors.

00:54:08.840 --> 00:54:11.810
So I'm not saying that
this is necessarily always

00:54:11.810 --> 00:54:14.603
a weight tool, but in some
context it shows like,

00:54:15.580 --> 00:54:18.189
I think it's between contexts

00:54:18.189 --> 00:54:21.103
where the health system in
may not be strong enough yet

00:54:21.103 --> 00:54:24.179
for people to feel that they can trust

00:54:24.179 --> 00:54:28.320
during an Ebola crisis, if I
go to the healthcare system,

00:54:28.320 --> 00:54:31.380
am I gonna get Ebola,
whatever I'm gonna be able

00:54:31.380 --> 00:54:35.410
to communicate information
about their own symptoms

00:54:35.410 --> 00:54:37.690
through a phone, for
example, would be a great way

00:54:37.690 --> 00:54:39.500
to help deal with crisis.

00:54:39.500 --> 00:54:42.400
So that's one extra area where
I think it could be useful,

00:54:42.400 --> 00:54:43.620
but I also wanna say,

00:54:43.620 --> 00:54:44.958
I don't think it's (indistinct)

00:54:44.958 --> 00:54:47.580
I got a phone call where Silicon valley,

00:54:47.580 --> 00:54:49.367
like every student comes and says,

00:54:49.367 --> 00:54:50.970
"Hey, I got this new app.

00:54:50.970 --> 00:54:52.810
That's gonna like solve
every problem on the planet."

00:54:52.810 --> 00:54:53.643
And I'm like,

00:54:53.643 --> 00:54:57.110
"No, I strongly doubt that."

00:54:57.110 --> 00:55:00.602
So I tend to have like an
outside technology point of view,

00:55:00.602 --> 00:55:03.984
just as a reaction to the average person

00:55:03.984 --> 00:55:05.230
around campus here.

00:55:05.230 --> 00:55:07.941
But so I just wanna say
there are a lot of areas

00:55:07.941 --> 00:55:10.440
where I think technology
is not gonna get us there.

00:55:10.440 --> 00:55:13.730
And I wanna bring us back
to comment I made earlier

00:55:13.730 --> 00:55:16.223
about the fact that they
are just like some diseases

00:55:16.223 --> 00:55:20.643
that we just don't yet have tools for.

00:55:21.540 --> 00:55:24.240
And malaria is one of them.

00:55:24.240 --> 00:55:25.300
And when it comes to vaccines,

00:55:25.300 --> 00:55:28.220
there is now some hope
that there is a vaccine

00:55:28.220 --> 00:55:30.695
that may be partially effective.

00:55:30.695 --> 00:55:34.110
And the question is how
do we make sure that

00:55:34.110 --> 00:55:39.110
this is all out widely
as quickly as possible.

