she's seen several openings
where there is a possibility

00:55:59.520 --> 00:56:01.960
for action to address health inequity,

00:56:01.960 --> 00:56:03.820
but then those moments come and go.

00:56:03.820 --> 00:56:06.690
And I wonder if you're optimistic

00:56:06.690 --> 00:56:08.702
that this moment might be any different.

00:56:08.702 --> 00:56:10.470
And if it is to be different,

00:56:10.470 --> 00:56:12.900
if real progress is to be
made, what needs to happen?

00:56:12.900 --> 00:56:15.400
So just quickly coming
back to each of you,

00:56:15.400 --> 00:56:16.790
how might this moment be different

00:56:16.790 --> 00:56:17.840
if you're at all optimistic,

00:56:17.840 --> 00:56:19.620
it can be and what needs to happen?

00:56:19.620 --> 00:56:21.370
Pascaline, can we start with you?

00:56:21.370 --> 00:56:24.840
- So, I mean, I just said
malaria vaccine as an example.

00:56:24.840 --> 00:56:27.183
Recently I saw an article by the CDG

00:56:28.055 --> 00:56:30.257
that I got very unhappy
with because it said,

00:56:30.257 --> 00:56:32.350
"Look, let's think twice
about this vaccine,

00:56:32.350 --> 00:56:34.930
is not as cost effective as other stuff."

00:56:34.930 --> 00:56:35.763
And it was like,

00:56:35.763 --> 00:56:38.650
"Oh, it's $200 per daily
compared to like 60

00:56:38.650 --> 00:56:39.483
for something else."

00:56:39.483 --> 00:56:41.757
And I'm like, "$200 for
daily, there's a bit here,

00:56:41.757 --> 00:56:42.590
this is left here."

00:56:42.590 --> 00:56:43.423
It's nothing like in the U.S

00:56:43.423 --> 00:56:46.110
we spent like thousands of
virus per days, every time.

00:56:46.110 --> 00:56:47.749
So it is super cost-effective

00:56:47.749 --> 00:56:51.247
and we just have to
move beyond just saying,

00:56:51.247 --> 00:56:53.020
"There's not enough
money and we have to go

00:56:53.020 --> 00:56:54.910
for the really, really,
really, really cheap stuff."

00:56:54.910 --> 00:56:57.560
We should have more
money available for that.

00:56:57.560 --> 00:56:59.950
And I think there's an
opening there because

00:56:59.950 --> 00:57:02.620
everybody here has seen what it means

00:57:02.620 --> 00:57:04.670
to be in an environment
where you are afraid

00:57:04.670 --> 00:57:07.170
of getting a disease from
your neighbor all the time.

00:57:07.170 --> 00:57:09.810
We know that we're willing
to make sacrifices,

00:57:09.810 --> 00:57:12.370
to just like, get rid of damn COVID, okay.

00:57:12.370 --> 00:57:13.920
Everybody has been doing that.

00:57:13.920 --> 00:57:16.260
And now we can put ourselves
in the shoes of people

00:57:16.260 --> 00:57:17.460
who have been living in-

00:57:23.210 --> 00:57:24.940
- Pascaline, you've just cut out for me.

00:57:24.940 --> 00:57:25.833
Can you hear us?

00:57:28.540 --> 00:57:30.330
Okay, I'm gonna go ahead and bring Mesfin

00:57:30.330 --> 00:57:31.730
and hopefully Pascaline connection

00:57:31.730 --> 00:57:33.380
will improve in one moment.

00:57:33.380 --> 00:57:35.087
- [Pascaline] For a year and a half.

00:57:37.840 --> 00:57:40.800
- Pascaline, I'm so sorry your
signal's cutting out for me.

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:42.680
Your signal cut out for me right there.

00:57:42.680 --> 00:57:44.330
But now I think we see
you, can you just wrap up,

00:57:44.330 --> 00:57:46.947
we missed the last little
bit of what you said.

00:57:46.947 --> 00:57:49.030
- Sorry, let's just move
on to the next panelist

00:57:49.030 --> 00:57:50.640
Sorry about my bad connection.

00:57:50.640 --> 00:57:52.120
- Oh, I was leaning in and so engaged

00:57:52.120 --> 00:57:52.953
in what you were saying,

00:57:52.953 --> 00:57:55.690
but I think I understand
where you were going with it.

00:57:55.690 --> 00:57:58.210
And I think we understood you enough

00:57:58.210 --> 00:57:59.043
to be able to build on that.

00:57:59.043 --> 00:58:01.350
So, Mesfin, can we go to you next?

00:58:01.350 --> 00:58:02.183
- Yeah, sure.

00:58:03.778 --> 00:58:06.280
When it comes to the greatest
challenge of all time,

00:58:06.280 --> 00:58:09.230
which we started with COVID-19 pandemic,

00:58:09.230 --> 00:58:11.083
as well as added climate change.

00:58:12.040 --> 00:58:13.827
As you know, everyone is talking about

00:58:13.827 --> 00:58:16.303
the need for building back better,

00:58:17.184 --> 00:58:20.220
but there is far too little attention

00:58:20.220 --> 00:58:24.160
in terms of the disparity
in terms of access to power

00:58:24.160 --> 00:58:27.170
and resource between high
and low income countries,

00:58:27.170 --> 00:58:28.670
even the most fragile states.

00:58:28.670 --> 00:58:32.020
So I do consider this as a blind spot

00:58:32.020 --> 00:58:34.990
to addressing the root
causes of injustice,

00:58:34.990 --> 00:58:38.605
whether it is vaccine
or humanitarian crisis.

00:58:38.605 --> 00:58:43.605
So, overlooking those context
is a strategic era as well.

00:58:45.510 --> 00:58:48.870
And I do think, if you want to be serious

00:58:48.870 --> 00:58:51.540
about building back better probably,

00:58:51.540 --> 00:58:53.610
for a world that we want to see

00:58:53.610 --> 00:58:55.233
not the one we left behind.

00:58:56.340 --> 00:59:01.270
We need to translate this
rhetoric into concrete action

00:59:01.270 --> 00:59:03.890
and through an inclusive
governance system,

00:59:03.890 --> 00:59:06.370
and which is accountable as well.

00:59:06.370 --> 00:59:09.183
And the pandemic has taught us that

00:59:09.183 --> 00:59:12.570
investment in public health
should be a public good,

00:59:12.570 --> 00:59:16.170
that government should not
delegate this responsibility

00:59:16.170 --> 00:59:17.590
to private sector.

00:59:17.590 --> 00:59:22.590
And we need to look at the
whole health governance system,

00:59:22.700 --> 00:59:25.380
including international health regulations

00:59:25.380 --> 00:59:28.200
for disease pandemic reporting.

00:59:28.200 --> 00:59:31.820
And so that really addressing those issues

00:59:31.820 --> 00:59:35.460
that are paralyzing now
to have a better world.

00:59:35.460 --> 00:59:37.040
I think we need to look at our governance

00:59:37.040 --> 00:59:39.803
and accountability mechanism
to have a fair world.

00:59:40.790 --> 00:59:41.748
- Thank you, Mesfin.

00:59:41.748 --> 00:59:43.721
And we're coming close to the end of time,

00:59:43.721 --> 00:59:45.730
but Anne-Emanuelle we would
love to hear your thoughts.

00:59:45.730 --> 00:59:49.580
- Yeah so I agree with
Mesfin that power asymmetries

00:59:49.580 --> 00:59:53.070
are the crucial issue of the day

00:59:53.070 --> 00:59:56.887
and of many, many years, in fact.

00:59:56.887 --> 01:00:00.930
And I wouldn't say optimistic, but hopeful

01:00:00.930 --> 01:00:04.160
in a range of actions
that have taken place

01:00:04.160 --> 01:00:06.070
in different parts of the world.

01:00:06.070 --> 01:00:09.185
The so-called striketober in North America

01:00:09.185 --> 01:00:13.840
attempts to unionize at Amazon in Edmonton

01:00:13.840 --> 01:00:17.610
and here in Canada, even
though it failed in Alabama,

01:00:17.610 --> 01:00:21.260
John Deere strikes, gig
worker strikes and so on

01:00:21.260 --> 01:00:25.740
all of the revelations
about corporate capitalism

01:00:25.740 --> 01:00:27.990
and so on extremely important.

01:00:27.990 --> 01:00:29.990
And together with those worker uprisings,

01:00:29.990 --> 01:00:33.740
there've been some notable
indigenous uprisings

01:00:33.740 --> 01:00:36.700
up and down the America's for example.

01:00:36.700 --> 01:00:41.120
I mean, Ecuadorean in
Cuenca passed a resolution,

01:00:41.120 --> 01:00:45.040
a referendum for clean
water and kicking out

01:00:45.040 --> 01:00:46.470
a Canadian mining company.

01:00:46.470 --> 01:00:49.390
That's a huge form of resistance.

01:00:49.390 --> 01:00:53.460
And I think something to be
hopeful for and build upon.

01:00:53.460 --> 01:00:58.180
Likewise elections in Peru
and finally in Bolivia

01:00:58.180 --> 01:01:01.120
overturning a repressive government

01:01:01.120 --> 01:01:03.510
that was supported by North America.

01:01:03.510 --> 01:01:05.380
So I think there are reasons

01:01:05.380 --> 01:01:10.380
for some hope and that is in the context

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:12.290
of people's struggles, right?

01:01:12.290 --> 01:01:17.290
Not in terms of experts
developing better technologies,

01:01:17.340 --> 01:01:19.600
divorced from all of that.

01:01:19.600 --> 01:01:20.433
- Thank you.

01:01:20.433 --> 01:01:23.670
Sunil, how are you feeling
as we close this conversation

01:01:23.670 --> 01:01:26.140
and how do we make sure that this moment

01:01:26.140 --> 01:01:29.150
doesn't just come and go
in terms of an opportunity

01:01:29.150 --> 01:01:30.643
to advance health equity?

01:01:31.990 --> 01:01:33.750
- One thing I've taken
from this conversation

01:01:33.750 --> 01:01:38.580
is the need for joined up thinking.

01:01:38.580 --> 01:01:41.040
We didn't talk much about climate change.

01:01:41.040 --> 01:01:42.690
That is very much been on my mind.

01:01:42.690 --> 01:01:44.420
And I think thinking about the ways

01:01:44.420 --> 01:01:48.640
in which new environmental
justice maps on to health equity

01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:51.420
I think without that
I don't think anything

01:01:51.420 --> 01:01:54.290
that we do will we will
succeed because in a sense,

01:01:54.290 --> 01:01:57.440
I think that COVID devastating though

01:01:57.440 --> 01:02:00.140
it has been relatively fleeting crisis

01:02:00.140 --> 01:02:01.710
compared with the climate crisis.

01:02:01.710 --> 01:02:03.460
And I think the ways
in which climate change

01:02:03.460 --> 01:02:06.720
is sort of supercharges all
of these existing inequalities

01:02:06.720 --> 01:02:10.270
is something that we
will have to confront.

01:02:10.270 --> 01:02:12.610
I wouldn't say I'm
optimistic, but nevertheless,

01:02:12.610 --> 01:02:15.620
I think, there are enough people

01:02:15.620 --> 01:02:17.850
in particularly young
people all over the world

01:02:17.850 --> 01:02:21.190
who are deeply about the climate crisis

01:02:21.190 --> 01:02:23.710
to make me think that if
ever there's going to be

01:02:23.710 --> 01:02:26.647
an opportunity to sort of
start to connect those dots,

01:02:26.647 --> 01:02:30.383
this might be as good as it
gets for the next little while.

01:02:31.280 --> 01:02:32.190
- Thank you, Sunil.

01:02:32.190 --> 01:02:33.960
Well, thank you all so
much for joining us.

01:02:33.960 --> 01:02:35.740
Apologies, we've been a
bit over on time there,

01:02:35.740 --> 01:02:38.270
but there was a lot of
complexity to get into.

01:02:38.270 --> 01:02:40.850
Again, this conversation
was part of a larger series,

01:02:40.850 --> 01:02:42.320
the Yale Development Dialogues.

